  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   This approach combines **sobol's random scrambling** with an adaptive **frequency-penalized correlation network** to construct solutions that exploit maximum frequency-of-occurrence relationships between h and 1-h. The core innovation is using **multi-periodic initialization** combined with **de-stricted reduced lattice potential** optimization that gradually relaxes constraints to escape local optima.\n\n   The null hypothesis is that current optimization gets trapped in deep valley because it's tightly enforcing the box constraints and integral constraint simultaneously. By temporarily relaxing these constraints with weighted probability and applying Sobol point-based initialization, we can explore more abstract structural relationships that global minimizers rely upon.\n\n   This addresses the primary bottleneck: low-probability escape from overlap-minimizing feature alignment. The method achieves an expected improvement because it uncovers patterns that alternating reduction and projection fail to discover.\n\n   Limitations: Requires careful handling of psuedo-random initialization and potential numerical instability. Theoretical guarantees focus on empirical dominance.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   Representation: Continuous vector h \u2208 [0,1]\u207f satisfying \u2211h = N/2 (where dx=2/0). \n\n   Objective: C5 = (2/N) * max\u2096corr(h,1-h)[k] (N=400-800 rotations)\n\n   Optimization: Minimizes C5 subject to:\n     0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i\n     \u2211h = N/2\n\n   Transformation: Define dual variable p and use penalized differences:\n        (1-h) = d(p) = sigmoid(-(\u03b1p + \u03b2))\n\n   Then we solve:\n        min_q max\u2096 sum_i (q_i * (1-d(p)[i\u00d7k])) \n   subject to \u2211q_i = N/2\n\n   With kernel K(q) = sum\u2096 exp(-\u03b1||q||) \n\n   Solve using: \n        h = d(p) with p = argmax\u209c K(t)\u22a5\u2207f(t) = 0\n\n   3. Implementation Procedure\n   a. Global initialization:\n        h\u2080 = random.uniform(0,1,N)\n        p\u2080 = uniform([-1,1],N)\n\n   b. Multi-stage randomization:\n        Stage1: Sobol scrambling with priority sampling:\n            h_sob = sobol_order(h\u2080, alpha=0.5)\n\n        Stage2: Penalized Gaussian peaks:\n            h_peak = apply_peak_filter(h_sob, std=10)\n\n   c. De-Focused Reduced Correlation Network (DFRCN):\n        Initialize \u03b3 = 1\n        For iter=1 to budget_t/5:\n            \u03b1 = \u03b10 * exp(-\u03b2 iter)\n            p = function_minimization(f=DFRCN_obj, x0=p)\n            h_temp = d(p) but with softmax bounds [1e-3,0.997]\n            if |||\u2207f(p)||/||p|| < tol: \n                Phase = 'Projection'\n            else:\n                Phase = 'Sobolization'\n\n   4. Search Choices\n        - Resolutions: [400,500,600] in auto-scaling mode\n        - Priority base: 27 large peaks per resolution\n        - Projection tolerance: 1e-10\n        - Penalization: \u03b1 from [0.001,2.0] following Cauchy\n\n   5. Budget and Robustness\n        Deadline checks at: \n            - Every 1/25 of budget\n            - After every two gradient descent steps\n        Fallback: If projected integral error ||p - p_true||_\u221e > 5%\n\n        Construction uses:\n            - Non-legacy random number for Sobol\n            - Early termination of projected gradient if no improvement\n\n   6. Validation Return \n        Contract ensures:\n            - integral |0.5 - sum(h)/N| < 1e-7\n            - 0 <= h[i] <= 1 + 1e-7 for all i\n            - C5 value stored separately\n\n        Temporary hold for:\n            correlation maxWidth in upper half = min(initialC, bestC)\n\n        Special handling:\n            If runtime exceeds budget_s, return best candidate found before deadline",
